Would you judge a whole movie from its opening scene? An income recorded while someone is studying, changing jobs, or raising a child can be an equally misleading preview. **How well do a mother’s earnings predict her child’s—and how much does the answer depend on which paycheck we measure?**

This tutorial uses SQL inside Python to link real families, construct comparable earnings measures, and estimate intergenerational elasticity (IGE). The surprise is that finding the right family is easier than finding the right measure of economic status.

## 1. Give every person the right key

I use the [NLSY79 and its Child/Young Adult survey](https://www.nlsinfo.org/investigator/). The extracts contain @@RAW_CHILDREN@@ children and @@RAW_MOTHERS@@ corresponding mothers. A child’s `C0000100` identifies the child; `C0000200` identifies the mother and matches `R0000100` in her file, as the [NLS linkage documentation](https://nlsinfo.org/content/cohorts/nlsy79-children/other-documentation/codebook-supplement/appendix-h-identification) explains. Siblings share a mother ID, so the relationship is many-to-one.

The snippets below explain the key steps; they are not a standalone script. Run `code/analysis.py` for the complete workflow, including income preparation and creation of the `matched` table used later.

Load the CSVs into SQLite, Python’s built-in SQL database:

```python
import sqlite3
import pandas as pd

con = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")
children = pd.read_csv("data/raw/children.csv").rename(columns={
    "C0000100": "child_id", "C0000200": "mother_id",
    "C0005700": "birth_year", "C0005400": "sex"
})
mothers = pd.read_csv("data/raw/mothers.csv").rename(columns={
    "R0000100": "mother_id", "R0000500": "birth_year"
})
mothers["birth_year"] += 1900  # Original years are 57–64.
assert children.child_id.is_unique
assert mothers.mother_id.is_unique
children.to_sql("children", con, index=False)
mothers.to_sql("mothers", con, index=False)
```

## 2. Match families, then check the join

`SELECT` chooses columns. `JOIN` connects rows whose mother IDs agree:

```python
con.executescript("""
CREATE TABLE matches AS
SELECT c.child_id, c.mother_id, c.birth_year,
       c.sex, m.birth_year AS mother_birth_year
FROM children AS c
JOIN mothers AS m ON c.mother_id = m.mother_id;
""")
assert con.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM matches").fetchone()[0] == len(children)
```

All children match. Checking unique keys first matters: duplicate mother rows could quietly multiply observations. We also aggregate each person’s income history **before** joining generations, preventing one mother-year from pairing with every child-year.

## 3. Turn paychecks into comparable income

Here, “income” means annual wage and salary earnings, not household income. The full script reshapes the histories, treats negative survey codes as missing, retains valid zeros, and converts earnings to 2019 dollars using [BLS CPI-U through FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/CPIAUCNS). Income-band answers are excluded because their category numbers are not dollars. Later questions use actual interview years; income-year age is measured at year-end.

Both generations’ averages use available observations within ages 30–39, requiring at least three income years. `GROUP BY` collects each child’s rows; `AVG` and `COUNT` perform aggregation:

```sql
SELECT child_id, AVG(real_income) AS child_avg,
       COUNT(DISTINCT income_year) AS child_years
FROM child_income
WHERE age BETWEEN 30 AND 39
GROUP BY child_id
HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT income_year) >= 3;
```

Apply the same operation to mothers. For the single-year comparison, select each person’s available year nearest age 35, breaking ties toward the earlier year. Requiring positive selected incomes and positive averages leaves **@@MAIN_N@@ children from @@MAIN_MOTHERS@@ mothers**, identical across both models. Zeros enter averages but cannot be logged as single-year earnings.

![Each dot represents one adult child; orange points summarize groups. Both generations’ earnings are averaged within their thirties. The fitted line describes association in this selected sample.](figures/01_matched_earnings.png){#fig-family fig-alt="Logarithmic scatterplot of mothers' and children's average annual earnings, with grouped geometric means and a fitted line."}

## 4. Estimate the elasticity, not economic destiny

Estimate

$$
\log(Y_{child})=\alpha+\beta\log(Y_{mother})+\varepsilon.
$$

The slope $\beta$ is the IGE: roughly, the percentage difference in children’s earnings associated with 1% higher maternal earnings. After SQL produces one row per child, Python fits the regression:

```python
import numpy as np

matched = pd.read_sql_query("SELECT * FROM matched", con)
X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(matched)), np.log(matched.mother_avg)])
alpha, beta = np.linalg.lstsq(X, np.log(matched.child_avg), rcond=None)[0]
```

The single-year estimate is **@@SINGLE@@**, versus **@@MULTI@@** using averages. The latter associates 10% higher maternal earnings with about **@@PERCENT@@% higher child earnings**. Averages contain @@M_YEARS@@ maternal and @@C_YEARS@@ child income years on average.

![Both estimates use the same children. Single years are selected near age 35; averages use at least three distinct years in ages 30–39. Intervals allow siblings’ errors to be correlated.](figures/02_single_vs_average.png){#fig-comparison fig-alt="Two IGE estimates compare one income year per generation with multiyear income averages, including 95 percent confidence intervals."}

The difference is @@GAP@@, with a mother-clustered 95% interval of [@@GAP_LOW@@, @@GAP_HIGH@@]. This sample does not establish a precise improvement. Averaging can reduce temporary fluctuations; it does not guarantee a larger estimate.

## 5. Change the camera angle: life-cycle bias

For @@LIFE_N@@ of these children, positive income is also observed near age 22 within ages 20–24. Holding their sample and mothers’ average income fixed gives an early-career slope of @@EARLY@@ and a thirties slope of @@LATE@@. These uncertain estimates cannot reveal the true lifetime IGE.

The mechanism is clearer in a numerical example. Suppose the true lifetime IGE is 0.40, but early earnings reveal only half of the differences in lifetime log earnings. The early slope is then $0.5\times0.40=0.20$. Midcareer earnings that fully reveal those differences produce 0.40. This is **life-cycle bias**, not just random paycheck noise: future high earners may still be training when measured young.

![Left: a same-child age comparison from the NLS data, holding maternal income fixed. Right: a hypothetical analytical demonstration with true IGE 0.40, not another empirical estimate. Averaging early-career earnings alone leaves the assumed age distortion intact.](figures/03_age_and_measurement.png){#fig-lifecycle fig-alt="Two panels distinguish uncertain observed age-specific slopes from a hypothetical example showing early-career slopes of 0.20 and a midcareer slope of 0.40."}

Averaging several years dampens short-lived shocks. Averaging only young years can retain the same age distortion, which is why the main comparison aligns both generations in their thirties. [Haider and Solon (2006)](https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.96.4.1308) document the broader age-dependent measurement problem.

These are unweighted associations in a selected sample with observed earnings. Attrition, excluded zeros, topcoding, and mothers’ labor-force participation limit generalization. Neither estimate is causal or a national mobility statistic.

## Code and reproducibility

The [GitHub project folder](https://github.com/ruibao1129/6400-rb/tree/main/blog/posts/post6) contains the tutorial source, Python/SQL code, figures, result tables, and README with data-extraction and replication instructions. Run `python code/analysis.py` to regenerate the analysis and article, then `quarto render index.qmd` to build the webpage. Detailed variable definitions follow the [NLS income guide](https://www.nlsinfo.org/content/cohorts/nlsy79-children/topical-guide/income/introduction) and downloaded codebooks.

**Takeaway:** SQL can find someone’s mother exactly; a paycheck describes economic status much less exactly. Match the family, align the life stage, and average enough income years before interpreting the slope as a story about opportunity.
